{\pard\qc\sa240\fs32\b Hepatoprotective and Metabolic Effects of {\i Bambusa vulgaris} and {\i Mangifera indica} Leaf Extracts in MASLD and Diabetes: A Preliminary Investigation and Secondary Data Synthesis Across Biochemical, Histological, and Molecular Endpoints\par}
{\pard\qc\sa120\fs23 \b Mabrig Korie\par}
{\pard\qc\sa120\fs18 Independent Researcher, Natural Products & Hepatometabolic Disease Research -- Mabrig Korie Ministries Research Division | MABRIG Digital Media, Port Harcourt, Rivers State, Nigeria\par}
{\pard\qc\sa120\fs18 contact@mabrigkorie.org | mabrigkorie.org\par}
{\pard\qc\sa120\fs19 Mabrig Journal of Interdisciplinary Research (MJIR), Vol. 2, Issue 1, 2026, pp. 185--208\par}
{\pard\qc\sa120\fs19 DOI: 10.5678/mjir.v2i1.009\par}
{\pard\qc\sa120\fs17 Received 13 June 2026 | Revised 16 June 2026 | Accepted 17 June 2026 | Published 18 June 2026\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b Abstract\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) and type 2 diabetes are rising sharply across sub-Saharan Africa, and two independently studied African and South Asian medicinal leaf extracts -- {\i Bambusa vulgaris} (common bamboo) and {\i Mangifera indica} (mango) -- each carry a documented evidentiary record relevant to both conditions. A 2026 in vivo and in silico study by Sarkar and Das reported that {\i B. vulgaris} leaf extract protected high-fat-diet rats against MASLD principally through AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) activation and peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma (PPAR-g) downregulation; an older but substantial literature on mangiferin-rich {\i M. indica} leaf extract documents antidiabetic, antioxidant, and hepatoprotective effects in diabetic and toxin-injury rodent models. This preliminary investigation takes those two literatures and re-organises them, endpoint by endpoint, around the precise set of biochemical, histological, and molecular outcomes that a combined hepatoprotective-and-metabolic efficacy study would be expected to report: hepatic biomarkers (ALT, AST, ALP), lipid profile (TC, TG, LDL-C, HDL-C), glycemic control (fasting blood glucose, insulin, HOMA-IR), liver and adipose histopathology, the molecular AMPK/PPAR-g axis, and the documented phytochemical composition underlying these effects.\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 {\b A methodological note governs everything that follows:} this is a preliminary investigation built on secondary data synthesis, not a new high-fat-diet or streptozotocin-induced animal study. No new rats were dosed, no new liver enzyme assay, lipid panel, oral glucose tolerance test, histological grading, Western blot, or GC-MS run was performed by the author. Every claim made about a specific endpoint is attributed to, and bounded by, what the cited secondary literature on {\i B. vulgaris} and {\i M. indica} already documents, using a deliberately conservative qualitative vocabulary rather than invented numerical values. Where the existing literature does not yet speak to a given endpoint for the combination, this is stated explicitly, and a staged roadmap toward the combined animal study that alone could supply genuinely new primary data is set out in full.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i Keywords\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 MASLD; Diabetes; Bambusa vulgaris; Mangifera indica; Mangiferin; AMPK; PPAR-g; HOMA-IR; Histopathology; Secondary Data Synthesis; Preliminary Investigation\par}
{\pard\box\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\brdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\sa200\sb200\fs21\i Methodological notice: This article is a preliminary investigation built on a secondary synthesis of previously published literature on {\i Bambusa vulgaris} and {\i Mangifera indica} leaf extracts, organised by documented endpoint category rather than around a proposed formulation. No new animal experiment, biochemical assay, histological grading, Western blot, or GC-MS run was performed by the author. Endpoint claims are attributed throughout to the cited secondary literature and use only qualitative documentation-strength language.\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b 1. Introduction: Reading the Documented Evidence Endpoint by Endpoint\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) -- the diagnosis that replaced non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) under the 2023 multisociety nomenclature revision (Rinella et al., 2023) -- now affects an estimated quarter to a third of adults worldwide (Younossi et al., 2016, 2018), frequently alongside, or progressing toward, type 2 diabetes. The two conditions share a common metabolic root -- insulin resistance, ectopic lipid accumulation, chronic low-grade inflammation -- and are increasingly treated in the literature as a single intertwined disease process rather than two separate diagnoses (Friedman, Neuschwander-Tetri, Rinella, & Sanyal, 2018). Across sub-Saharan Africa, where nutrition transition and rising obesity are accelerating the prevalence of both, the case for affordable, locally available, plant-derived interventions is not a matter of taste but of access: the pharmacological agents with the strongest trial evidence, such as the GLP-1 receptor agonist semaglutide, remain priced out of reach for most patients in the region (Newsome et al., 2021; Paik et al., 2020).\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Two plants carry an unusually well-documented evidentiary record against exactly this disease cluster. A 2026 study by Sarkar and Das reported that daily oral {\i Bambusa vulgaris} leaf extract protected high-fat-diet rats against MASLD, with a published mechanistic resolution that is genuinely unusual for the ethnopharmacological literature: a clear, reported increase in phosphorylated AMPK and a clear, reported decrease in PPAR-g expression in liver and adipose tissue, alongside improved liver histology and an in silico docking component implicating specific identified flavonoids (Sarkar & Das, 2026). Independently, a substantial body of published work on {\i Mangifera indica} leaf extract and its principal xanthone, mangiferin, documents antidiabetic, antioxidant, and hepatoprotective activity across several diabetic and toxin-induced liver-injury rodent models, with reported improvements in glycemic parameters, lipid profile, and liver histology (Muruganandan, Srinivasan, Gupta, Gupta, & Lal, 2005; Nadella et al., 2016; Jariyapongskul et al., 2025).\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 This journal has already published a synthesis that reads these two literatures together to propose a novel quadriherbal formulation, with {\i B. vulgaris} and {\i M. indica} as its core two components and two further African herbs left as candidates for future selection (Korie, 2026a). That earlier article asked a formulation question: what should be combined, and in what proportion. This article asks a different, narrower, and in some ways more basic question, left largely implicit in that earlier synthesis: endpoint by endpoint -- hepatic biomarkers, lipid profile, glycemic control, histopathology, the molecular AMPK/PPAR-g axis, and the phytochemical record -- exactly how much does the existing published literature on these two plants already document, and where, specifically, does that documentation run out? Answering that question carefully is the precondition for designing any combined validation study, whether or not a third or fourth herb is eventually added.\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The motivation for organising the synthesis this way, rather than around a formulation, is practical. A researcher designing a combined {\i B. vulgaris}--{\i M. indica} animal study -- whether a high-fat-diet MASLD model, a streptozotocin-induced diabetic model, or a combined HFD-and-STZ model -- needs to know, endpoint by endpoint, which outcome measures already have strong single-plant precedent and which would represent a genuinely novel measurement for this combination. Liver enzymes, lipid panels, fasting glucose and HOMA-IR, histological grading, Western blot of the AMPK/PPAR-g axis, and GC-MS-based phytochemical profiling are the standard outcome categories such a study would report; this article synthesises what is already known, category by category, against exactly that template.\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Five tasks organise what follows. First, the documented dataset behind each plant individually is summarised. Second, the secondary sources and the endpoint-category framework used to organise the synthesis are specified, together with a complete protocol for a future combined validation study. Third, the synthesis itself is presented, endpoint by endpoint. Fourth, the pattern that emerges -- where documentation is strong and where it is thin -- is discussed plainly. Fifth, a staged roadmap from this preliminary synthesis to a genuinely informative combined animal study is set out.\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b 2. Background\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 2.1 MASLD and Diabetes as Intertwined Metabolic Disorders\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 MASLD is now diagnosed by the presence of hepatic steatosis together with at least one cardiometabolic risk factor, reflecting consensus recognition that the disease is a hepatic manifestation of systemic metabolic dysfunction rather than an isolated liver complaint (Rinella et al., 2023; Eslam et al., 2020). The same insulin resistance and lipotoxic environment that drives hepatic steatosis is also the proximate driver of type 2 diabetes, and the two conditions co-occur often enough in clinical populations that interventions addressing one are routinely evaluated for effects on the other (Friedman et al., 2018). Any candidate plant extract with documented activity on the AMPK/PPAR-g axis, the incretin axis, or systemic oxidative stress is therefore plausibly relevant to both conditions at once, which is precisely the documented profile that makes {\i B. vulgaris} and {\i M. indica} worth reading together.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 2.2 Bambusa vulgaris: The Documented Hepatoprotective and Metabolic Dataset\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The published record on {\i B. vulgaris} leaf extract in MASLD rests substantially on the 2026 Sarkar and Das study, which reported, in high-fat-diet rats given daily oral extract for four weeks: attenuated body-weight gain relative to untreated controls; improved liver histology with reduced steatosis; increased phosphorylated, activated AMPK and decreased PPAR-g expression in liver and adipose tissue; and an in silico docking component implicating the C-glycosylflavones isoorientin and vitexin as plausible contributors to the observed effects (Sarkar & Das, 2026). This is a single primary source rather than a converging multi-study literature, a limitation discussed explicitly in Section 5.3, but its mechanistic resolution -- a named, plausible molecular pathway rather than a generic \ldblquote hepatoprotective\rdblquote  label -- is unusual enough in this literature to anchor the synthesis that follows.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 2.3 Mangifera indica: The Documented Antidiabetic and Hepatoprotective Dataset\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The {\i M. indica} literature is older and broader, spanning multiple independent research groups. Mangiferin and standardized mango leaf extract have been reported to reduce hyperglycemia and improve atherogenic lipid indices in streptozotocin-induced diabetic rats (Muruganandan et al., 2005); to improve liver function markers in extract-treated animals (Nadella et al., 2016); and, in more recent work, to stimulate GLP-1 secretion via MAPK- and Wnt-linked signalling in enteroendocrine cells, improving glycaemic control and insulin sensitivity in diabetic rodent models while also exerting antioxidant and hepatoprotective effects (Jariyapongskul et al., 2025). Taken together, this is a more extensive and more consistently reported dataset than the single-study {\i B. vulgaris} record, but it documents a different primary mechanism -- incretin enhancement and antioxidant defence -- rather than the AMPK/PPAR-g axis foregrounded in the bamboo literature.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 2.4 Why an Endpoint-Organised Synthesis, Distinct from Formulation Design\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The earlier MJIR synthesis of this pairing was organised around a proposed quadriherbal product: which plants, in what proportion, for what purpose (Korie, 2026a). That framing is useful for product development but tends to compress the underlying endpoint-level evidence into a single mechanistic narrative. This article instead holds the two plants apart and re-reads their documented findings against the specific outcome categories -- hepatic biomarkers, lipid profile, glycemic markers, histopathology, molecular markers, and phytochemical identification -- that any combined validation study, with or without additional herbs, would need to report. This is the more basic evidentiary accounting that a formulation proposal presupposes but does not itself spell out in full.\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b 3. Methods: Secondary Sources, Endpoint Framework, and a Protocol for Future Validation\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 No animals were dosed, no human participants were recruited, and no new biochemical, histological, molecular, or phytochemical assay of any kind was run in the course of this work. Every claim below originates in already-published, peer-reviewed literature on {\i B. vulgaris} or {\i M. indica}. The contribution made here is the endpoint-by-endpoint organisation of that literature and the specification of a protocol for the combined study it implies.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.1 Sources Consulted\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The anchor source for {\i B. vulgaris} is the 2026 Sarkar and Das study (PMID: 41727251), from which dosing regimen (200--400 mg/kg orally, daily, four weeks), animal model details, and the full set of reported biochemical, histological, and molecular outcomes were extracted. The {\i M. indica} literature comprises Muruganandan et al. (2005), Nadella et al. (2016), and Jariyapongskul et al. (2025), supplemented by the broader MASLD and diabetes literature used to contextualise disease mechanism and burden (Rinella et al., 2023; Eslam et al., 2020; Friedman et al., 2018; Younossi et al., 2016, 2018; Newsome et al., 2021; Paik et al., 2020). A companion MJIR article proposing a quadriherbal formulation built from the same two anchor plants is cited where relevant for cross-reference, but its formulation-design content is not repeated here (Korie, 2026a).\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.2 Endpoint Categories Used to Organise the Synthesis\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Six endpoint categories, corresponding to the outcome measures a combined hepatoprotective-and-metabolic efficacy study would standardly report, structure Section 4: hepatic biomarkers (ALT, AST, ALP); lipid profile (total cholesterol, triglycerides, LDL-C, HDL-C); glycemic control (fasting blood glucose, insulin, HOMA-IR); histopathology (steatosis, inflammation, and fibrosis grading in liver and adipose tissue); molecular markers (AMPK phosphorylation, PPAR-g expression); and phytochemical identification (documented compound classes underlying the above effects). For each category, the synthesis states what is documented for {\i B. vulgaris}, what is documented for {\i M. indica}, and, where applicable, what is not yet documented for either.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.3 Evidentiary Standards Applied\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Claims are presented using a deliberately conservative, qualitative vocabulary -- \ldblquote well documented,\rdblquote  \ldblquote reported,\rdblquote  \ldblquote limited evidence reviewed\rdblquote  -- calibrated to how directly and how consistently a given endpoint is addressed in the cited source literature, rather than to any new computation. No invented numerical value for ALT, AST, triglycerides, HOMA-IR, or any other measure is reported anywhere in Section 4, because no new assay producing such a value was performed for this article; where a source study's published outcome is described in directional terms (\ldblquote reported reductions,\rdblquote  \ldblquote improved histology\rdblquote ), this reflects the source's own published finding rather than a number generated by the present synthesis.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.4 A Protocol for a Future Combined HFD/STZ Validation Study\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The remainder of this section specifies, as a prospective protocol rather than a description of work performed, the design a future combined-extract validation study would need.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.4.1 Animal Model and Induction\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 A combined high-fat-diet (for MASLD) and, if diabetes-relevant endpoints are also sought, low-dose streptozotocin-induced (for hyperglycemia) rodent model, run alongside a normal-diet control group and single-extract comparator groups for each of {\i B. vulgaris} and {\i M. indica} individually, would be required to isolate any combination-specific effect from what each plant already documents alone.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.4.2 Dosing and Treatment Duration\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Dosing should be anchored to the regimens already reported in the source literature -- approximately 200--400 mg/kg/day for {\i B. vulgaris} extract (Sarkar & Das, 2026) and comparable extract-equivalent dosing for {\i M. indica}, informed by Muruganandan et al. (2005), Nadella et al. (2016), and Jariyapongskul et al. (2025) -- administered for a treatment duration of at least four weeks to allow comparison against the existing single-plant datasets.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.4.3 Biochemical, Histological, and Molecular Endpoints\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The six endpoint categories specified in Section 3.2 define the measurement plan: serum ALT, AST, and ALP; a full lipid panel (TC, TG, LDL-C, HDL-C); fasting blood glucose, fasting insulin, and HOMA-IR, alongside an oral glucose tolerance test; histological steatosis, inflammation, and fibrosis scoring of liver tissue and adipocyte morphology of adipose tissue, ideally using an established scoring system such as the NAFLD Activity Score (Kleiner et al., 2005); and Western blot quantification of the p-AMPK/AMPK ratio and PPAR-g expression in both liver and adipose tissue, with relevant downstream markers (ACC, SREBP-1c) included where feasible.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.4.4 Phytochemical Profiling\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 GC-MS or HPLC profiling of the combined extract, benchmarked against the individually profiled single-plant extracts, would identify which documented bioactive compounds -- isoorientin and vitexin from bamboo; mangiferin and associated flavonoids from mango -- are present at comparable concentrations in the combined preparation, and would flag any unexpected compound interactions arising specifically from co-extraction or co-administration.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 3.4.5 Statistical Analysis Plan\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 A pre-specified analysis plan comparing the combined-extract group against each single-extract group and against untreated and normal-diet controls, using appropriate one-way or two-way ANOVA with post-hoc correction for multiple comparisons across the six endpoint categories, would be needed to determine whether the combination produces additive, synergistic, or merely comparable effects relative to either plant alone -- a question this secondary synthesis cannot answer by construction.\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b 4. Results: Synthesis of Documented Evidence by Endpoint Category\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 {\b Every claim in this section synthesises previously published literature; none derives from a new assay run by the author.} The synthesis below is organised by the six endpoint categories specified in Section 3.2, first for {\i B. vulgaris}, then for {\i M. indica}, closing with a cross-endpoint summary table.\par}
