{\pard\sa180\fs18\i Endpoint categories listed reflect what each cited source reports for its own single-plant or single-compound study; no row in this table describes the combined extract, which has not been studied.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.1 Hepatic Biomarkers (ALT, AST, ALP)\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 For {\i B. vulgaris}, Sarkar and Das (2026) reported improved liver-function status alongside the histological and molecular changes described below, consistent with a hepatoprotective effect, though the published emphasis of that study sits more heavily on histology and molecular markers than on a fully itemised enzyme panel. For {\i M. indica}, Nadella et al. (2016) specifically reported improved liver function markers in extract-treated animals relative to toxin-injured controls, the more direct hepatic-biomarker evidence of the two source literatures. Taken together, hepatic-biomarker improvement is reported for both plants individually; well documented for {\i M. indica}, reported for {\i B. vulgaris}.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.2 Lipid Profile (TC, TG, LDL-C, HDL-C)\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Muruganandan et al. (2005) reported that mangiferin reduced atherogenic lipid indices in streptozotocin-diabetic rats, directly addressing this endpoint category for {\i M. indica}. The {\i B. vulgaris} literature documents improved lipid metabolism as a downstream consequence of AMPK activation and PPAR-g downregulation (Sarkar & Das, 2026; Hardie, 2011; Tontonoz & Spiegelman, 2008), which is a mechanistically grounded inference rather than an independently itemised TC/TG/LDL-C/HDL-C panel in the cited source. Lipid-profile improvement is therefore well documented for {\i M. indica} and reported, via mechanism, for {\i B. vulgaris}.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.3 Glycemic Control (FBG, Insulin, HOMA-IR)\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 This is the endpoint category with the strongest documentation for {\i M. indica}: Muruganandan et al. (2005) reported reduced hyperglycemia in streptozotocin-diabetic rats, and Jariyapongskul et al. (2025) reported improved glycemic control and insulin sensitivity via GLP-1-related signalling in diabetic rodent models -- together constituting well-documented evidence across two independent groups. {\i B. vulgaris}, by contrast, was studied in a MASLD rather than a diabetes model, and the Sarkar and Das (2026) source does not report fasting insulin or HOMA-IR directly; glycemic relevance for bamboo extract is inferred from AMPK's well-documented general role in glucose handling (Hardie, 2011; Garcia & Shaw, 2017) rather than from a directly reported HOMA-IR value. Glycemic control is therefore well documented for {\i M. indica} and only indirectly, mechanistically reported for {\i B. vulgaris}.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.4 Histopathology (Steatosis, Inflammation, Fibrosis)\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 {\i B. vulgaris} has the stronger direct documentation here: Sarkar and Das (2026) reported measurably improved liver histology, with reduced hepatic steatosis, in extract-treated high-fat-diet rats. {\i M. indica} extract has been reported to preserve liver architecture in toxin-induced injury models (Nadella et al., 2016), a related but distinct histological context (acute toxin injury rather than diet-induced steatosis) from the MASLD model used for bamboo. Histopathological improvement is well documented for {\i B. vulgaris} in a MASLD-relevant model and reported for {\i M. indica} in a different (toxin-injury) histological context.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.5 Molecular Markers (AMPK Phosphorylation, PPAR-g Expression)\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 This endpoint category is, by a clear margin, where the {\i B. vulgaris} literature is strongest: Sarkar and Das (2026) reported a direct, measured increase in phosphorylated AMPK and a direct, measured decrease in PPAR-g expression in both liver and adipose tissue, the single most mechanistically specific finding in either source literature. The {\i M. indica} literature consulted here does not report AMPK or PPAR-g measurements directly; its documented mechanism runs instead through GLP-1/incretin signalling (Jariyapongskul et al., 2025). The AMPK/PPAR-g axis is therefore well documented for {\i B. vulgaris} and not documented (on the sources consulted here) for {\i M. indica} -- a genuine mechanistic complementarity rather than a gap in the synthesis.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.6 Phytochemical Identification (Documented Compound Classes)\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The in silico component of Sarkar and Das (2026) identified the C-glycosylflavones isoorientin and vitexin as plausible contributors to the bamboo extract's activity. The {\i M. indica} literature consistently identifies mangiferin, a C-glycosyl xanthone, as the principal documented bioactive, alongside associated flavonoids and phenolic acids (Muruganandan et al., 2005; Jariyapongskul et al., 2025). Both compound classes are well documented in the public phytochemical literature generally (PubChem, ChEMBL-indexed structures); no new GC-MS run was performed for either extract or for any combined preparation as part of this article, and no GC-MS peak data, retention time, or relative-abundance percentage is reported here for that reason.\par}
