{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 4.7 The Pattern That Emerges\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Read across Table 2, the two plants' documented strengths are complementary rather than overlapping: {\i B. vulgaris} is best documented on histopathology and the AMPK/PPAR-g molecular axis, while {\i M. indica} is best documented on glycemic control and hepatic biomarkers. Neither plant's existing literature directly addresses the full six-category set, and no source addresses any endpoint for the two plants administered together. This complementary-but-incomplete pattern is precisely the evidentiary gap that the protocol in Section 3.4 is designed to close.\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b 5. Discussion\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 5.1 What an Endpoint-Organised Synthesis Adds\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Organising the existing {\i B. vulgaris} and {\i M. indica} literature by outcome category, rather than by plant or by proposed formulation, surfaces a specific, actionable gap that a more narrative synthesis can obscure: the two plants' documented strengths sit on almost entirely different endpoints, which means a combined validation study cannot simply average or extrapolate from either single-plant dataset -- it would need to measure all six categories directly, in the same animals, to know whether the documented strengths of each plant actually transfer when combined. This complements, rather than duplicates, the formulation-design synthesis published earlier in this journal (Korie, 2026a), which addressed composition and rationale but did not itemise the endpoint-level evidentiary base in this much detail.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 5.2 Where Documentation Is Strong Versus Where It Is Thin\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The strongest single finding in either literature is the directly measured AMPK/PPAR-g result for {\i B. vulgaris} (Sarkar & Das, 2026); the broadest, most independently replicated finding is the glycemic and lipid effect of mangiferin across multiple {\i M. indica} studies (Muruganandan et al., 2005; Jariyapongskul et al., 2025). The thinnest documentation, on the sources consulted here, is glycemic control for {\i B. vulgaris} (inferred from AMPK biology rather than directly measured) and the AMPK/PPAR-g axis for {\i M. indica} (not addressed at all in the sources reviewed). Neither gap implies the effect is absent; it implies that the specific measurement has not yet been published for that plant, which is a different and more answerable problem.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 5.3 Limitations\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 The {\i B. vulgaris} synthesis rests on a single primary source (Sarkar & Das, 2026); no second, independent replication of its specific AMPK/PPAR-g findings was identified in the literature consulted, and any single-study finding carries more uncertainty than a multiply replicated one. The {\i M. indica} literature, while broader, is studied across different specific models (diabetic, toxin-injury) rather than a single consistent MASLD model, which limits direct cross-comparison with the bamboo dataset. No source addresses the two plants in combination on any endpoint, so every statement in Section 4 about the combination's plausible behaviour is an inference from single-plant data, not a documented combination effect. Publication bias toward positive rodent findings is an endemic risk in this literature and cannot be ruled out for any cited study. These limitations define, rather than undermine, the specific work the protocol in Section 3.4 is designed to do.\par}
{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 5.4 Staged Roadmap to a Combined Validation Study\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 Table 3 sets out the sequence of work that would convert this endpoint-organised secondary synthesis into genuine combined-extract evidence.\par}
