{\pard\sa100\sb180\fs23\b\i 5.5 Relevance to Nigeria and the Wider Region\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 {\i B. vulgaris} and {\i M. indica} are both common, inexpensive, and widely available across Nigeria and much of West Africa, which matters as much for the affordability of any future combined extract as for the affordability of the validation research itself. An endpoint-organised synthesis of this kind is a low-cost step that any research group, including those without immediate access to animal-house or Western blot facilities, can use to prioritise which specific outcome measure to pursue first -- for instance, prioritising a glycemic-control study of bamboo extract (the documentation gap identified in Section 4.3) over re-confirming an already well-documented mangiferin finding.\par}
{\pard\sa120\sb240\fs26\b 6. Conclusion\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 This preliminary investigation reorganises the documented {\i Bambusa vulgaris} and {\i Mangifera indica} literature around six endpoint categories -- hepatic biomarkers, lipid profile, glycemic control, histopathology, molecular markers, and phytochemical identification -- that a combined hepatoprotective-and-metabolic validation study would need to report. The synthesis finds genuine, complementary documented strength: {\i B. vulgaris} is well documented on histopathology and the AMPK/PPAR-g molecular axis, while {\i M. indica} is well documented on glycemic control and hepatic biomarkers, with neither plant's existing literature covering the full set and no source addressing the two in combination on any endpoint.\par}
{\pard\sa180\sb0\fs22 No new animal experiment, biochemical assay, histological grading, Western blot, or GC-MS run was performed for this article, and the work makes no efficacy claim for any combined extract. What it offers is a citable accounting of exactly where the existing evidence stands, endpoint by endpoint, and a staged roadmap (Table 3) toward the combined study that alone could confirm whether these two well-documented but separately studied plants behave as expected when given together -- a specific, answerable, and comparatively inexpensive next step for a Nigerian or other African research group equipped to run it.\par}
